  "Ramesses II reigned from 1279 to 1213 BCE. [[c-ramesses]] He built a new capital in the eastern Delta, Pi-Ramesses, [[c-piramesse]] and covered Egypt with temples. Around 1274 BCE he marched north and met the Hittite king Muwatalli II at Kadesh in Syria. His temples proclaim a great victory; in fact the Hittites kept Kadesh, and historians judge the battle a draw. [[c-kadesh]] About fifteen years later Ramesses and the Hittite king Hattusili III made a treaty of peace and alliance, preserved in both Egyptian and Akkadian. [[c-treaty]] It is often called the first peace treaty in history; older ones are known. [[c-treaty-first]]",
  "About 1208 BCE, his son Merneptah set up a stele boasting of victories in Canaan, naming places such as Ashkelon and Gezer, and declaring: 'Israel is wasted, bare of seed.' Israel is written with the sign for a people, not a city or land. [[c-merneptah]] It is generally regarded as the earliest secure mention of Israel outside the Bible. [[c-merneptah-first]]",
